==German==
[[File:Mädchen 1.jpg|thumb|right|220px|Ein '''Mädchen''']]
[[File:Bundesarchiv Bild 194-0109-24A, Urdenbach, Betreuung von Flüchtlingskindern.jpg|thumb|right|Two German girls in the children refugee care center in Urdenbach, Germany in the immediate post-Second World War period]]

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|de|Mägdchen||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|magedichīn}}, {{m|gmh|meidichīn}}, from {{inh|de|goh|*magadihhīn}} or calqued from {{cal|de|gml|mēgedeken|notext=1}}, {{m|gml|megedekīn}}, both from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*magaþukīn}}. Equivalent to {{af|de|Magd|-chen}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈmɛːtçən/|aa=Germany,prescriptive; used naturally in western Germany}}
* {{IPA|de|/ˈmeːtçən/|aa=Germany,overall more common; particularly northern and eastern regions}}
** {{audio|de|De-Mädchen.ogg|aa=Germany}}
** {{audio|de|De-Mädchen2.ogg|aa=<<Germany>> (<<Berlin>>)}}
** {{rhyme|de|ɛːtçən|s=2}}
* {{IPA|de|/ˈmeːtçɛn/|aa=Austria,South German}}
* {{IPA|de|/ˈmɛːtçɛn/|aa=Switzerland}}

===Noun===
{{de-noun|n,s,-:s[nonstandard, uncommon]}}

# [[girl]]
# [[maiden]]

====Usage notes====
* Since {{m|de||Mädchen}} is a grammatically neuter noun, all preceding articles, determiners, and adjectives take neuter forms: ''<u>ein</u> klein<u>es</u> Mädchen'' (“a little girl”). This rule is followed throughout all registers of German.
* There is more variation concerning pronouns referring back to ''Mädchen''. These are usually neuter within the same sentence: ''Das Mädchen, <u>das</u> eben hier war, hat <u>seine</u> Tasche vergessen.'' – “The girl who was just here, forgot her purse.” (But feminine {{m|de|ihre}} would be acceptable in colloquial usage.) It is quite common, however, to use feminine pronouns in following sentences: ''Siehst du das Mädchen, <u>das</u> da steht? Kennst du <u>die</u>?'' – “Do you see the girl who [''neuter''] is standing there? Do you know her [''feminine'']?” (But neuter {{m|de|es}} would be acceptable in literary usage.)
* {{m|de||Mädchen}} is typically used to refer to young women up to the age of 30. Past this age it is uncommon (unlike {{cog|en|girl}}) – the related term {{m|de|Mädel}} is more widely used colloquially for adult women.
* The plural {{m|de|Mädchens}} is archaic. Compare colloquial {{m|de|Jungs}}.

====Declension====
{{de-ndecl|n,s,-:s[nonstandard, uncommon]}}

====Synonyms====
* {{l|de|Mädel|g=n}} {{q|colloquial}}
* {{l|de|Mädken}}, {{l|de|Mädle|g=n}} {{q|regional}}

====Antonyms====
* {{q|by gender}} {{l|de|Bub|g=m}}, {{l|de|Bube|g=m}} {{q|Austrian|Swiss}}, {{l|de|Junge|g=m}}, {{l|de|Knabe|g=m}}
